bpf: Cache maintenance instructions

More and more BPF deployments run on memory that is not fully coherent
with respect to every agent that touches it:

  - SoCs whose DMA-capable engines, NPUs or crypto/compression
    accelerators sit behind a non-cache-coherent interconnect;
  - descriptor rings and data buffers shared with SmartNICs, FPGAs and
    other devices that read/write host memory directly;
  - CXL-attached and persistent memory, where the point of coherency and
    the point of persistence differ and software must control when data
    becomes visible/durable.

On such systems software has to perform explicit cache maintenance
around the point where a non-coherent agent accesses the memory: clean
(write back) before handing a buffer to the device, and invalidate
before reading a buffer the device has written. Today a BPF program has
no way to express this. The options are to route through a helper or a
bespoke kfunc per platform, or to move the logic out of BPF entirely,
both of which defeat the point of doing the work in BPF in the first
place (XDP/AF_XDP fast paths, tracing on the DMA completion path,
struct_ops data-plane callbacks, ...).

This series extends the BPF instruction set with three cache maintenance
instructions, so the coherency operations can be expressed directly and
JITed to a single native instruction.

Encoding
========

The instructions reuse the BPF_ATOMIC mode of the STX class, following
the scheme established by load-acquire/store-release. The operation is
encoded in the immediate field and only the BPF_B size modifier is used:

  {ATOMIC, B, STX}, imm = 0x120   BPF_CACHE_INVAL   invalidate block
  {ATOMIC, B, STX}, imm = 0x130   BPF_CACHE_CLEAN   write back block
  {ATOMIC, B, STX}, imm = 0x140   BPF_CACHE_FLUSH   write back + invalidate

All three operate on the implementation-defined cache maintenance block
containing dst_reg + off16. src_reg is reserved (must be 0) and no
register is written. The operations are self-ordering: the maintenance
completes before any subsequent memory access of the program, so no
separate barrier instruction is needed. Programs walk larger regions by
iterating in steps of the cache maintenance block size.

Semantics and safety
====================

  - INVAL makes memory visible to subsequent reads. Implementations that
    can only clean+invalidate (e.g. x86 CLFLUSH) are allowed, so a
    program must not rely on INVAL discarding un-written-back data.
  - CLEAN writes dirty data back; the block may stay resident.
  - FLUSH writes back and then invalidates.

The verifier treats all three as writes to the addressed memory (INVAL
can drop not-yet-written-back stores), and only accepts writable,
non-stack pointers that are not ctx/packet/socket/flow-key/arena.
Because selectively evicting cache lines is a building block for
cache-timing side channels (flush+reload and friends), the instructions
require a privileged program (CAP_PERFMON).

Support is optional and architecture dependent; an architecture opts in
with bpf_jit_supports_cache_ops() and bpf_arch_cache_op(). The verifier
rejects the instructions at load time everywhere else.

Backends
========

  - x86-64: CLFLUSH + MFENCE. CLFLUSH is write-back+invalidate, a valid
    (stronger) implementation of all three ops; MFENCE provides the
    required ordering. The interpreter uses clflush_cache_range().
  - arm64: DC IVAC / DC CVAC / DC CIVAC to the point of coherency, plus
    DSB SY. The interpreter uses the dcache_*_poc() helpers.

Arena pointers are not supported yet and are rejected by the verifier
via bpf_jit_supports_insn(); this can be relaxed later.

Open questions / future work
============================

  - libbpf/LLVM assembler mnemonics (cache_inval/clean/flush) so the
    instructions are directly writable from C; this series only wires up
    the kernel side and __imm_insn()-based selftests.
  - Batched/weaker variants (x86 CLFLUSHOPT/CLWB, arm64 without the
    trailing DSB) for programs that flush a range and issue one barrier
    at the end.
  - Point-of-persistence variants for pmem/CXL use cases.
  - A kfunc wrapper for a range-based operation may complement the
    per-block instruction for large buffers.

Feedback on the encoding, the privilege model and the ordering guarantee
is very welcome before this grows a stable UAPI.

